frq:{prompt:'Evaluate Dr. Morales’s fictional argument: identify its claim, evidence and warrant; explain how she handles the counterargument; and state one limitation of the evidence.',rubric:[['Identify claim, evidence and warrant',['claim','evidence','warrant','own']],['Explain the counterargument and response',['burnout','time','modif','counterargument']],['State a limitation',['one case','cost','uncertain','limit']]],model:'Dr. Morales claims that every discharged patient should have a named clinician responsible for pending results. Her evidence is a case in which a biopsy result went unread for weeks, and her warrant is that results are missed because no one owns them, so assigning ownership should reduce misses. The administrator counters that added duties without time will burn out staff; she accepts the concern and modifies the proposal by counting follow-up work in schedules. A limitation is that one case cannot show how often results are missed or prove that ownership alone fixes the problem, and she admits she does not know the cost.'}
